   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "There is no explicit roll here, and that is the point of hedging per contract. The May WTI\n",
    "hedge only covers the fixings that use May WTI, so it fades day by day and is closed by\n",
    "20 April, the day May WTI stops trading (earlier here, once less than half a lot is left). The June position covers the rest of the month. A\n",
    "desk rolls when it hedges in a different month than the exposure, for instance everything in\n",
    "the most liquid contract, and then moves the position with a calendar spread before expiry."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c05f5857",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. P&L: options alone vs hedged book\n",
    "\n",
    "Day 0 is at fair value, so the P&L starts at 0. Option P&L is the change in PV plus the payoffs\n",
    "when they are paid. Hedge P&L is the variation margin on the futures held overnight."
   ]
  },
